Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 03:08:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] O5: oracle per-frame recorder over the P1 socket
 (GUAC_ORACLE=1)

Three verbs on P1's AF_UNIX listener so the oracle records every frame of a run without pausing it (openspec AG.1, design decision 6). Behind O3's gate: each answers P1's ERR unknown_cmd unless GUAC_ORACLE=1 is in the emulator's environment. Nothing knows the game: the client names the guest ranges, and every flip is RSX's int_flip_index, the counter STATE and HB report.

- WATCH <frames> <addr> <len> [<addr> <len> ...] -> OK bytes=<b> frames=<n> | ERR bad_args | ERR bad_frames | ERR bad_range | ERR too_big | ERR no_renderer | ERR unmapped. Arms a ring of <frames> records (1 to 1,048,576), each the ranges' bytes back to back: up to 64 ranges, addresses hex, lengths decimal, 16,384 bytes a record, 16 MiB for the ring with its headers. Every range has to be readable when it's armed. Replaces any watch before it.
- WATCH_READ [<first> <count>] -> OK held=<h> total=<t> bytes=<b> frames=<n> armed=<flip> | OK first=<i> count=<k> data=<hex> | ERR bad_args | ERR not_paused | ERR no_watch | ERR bad_index | ERR bad_count. Only at a pause, so the ring holds still from one read to the next. Without arguments the summary: records held, taken since arming (the difference is what the ring overwrote), a record's payload, the ring's size and the counter when it was armed. With them those records, 0 the oldest held, each a 24-byte header (the flip, the host's steady clock in microseconds, a mask of the ranges that weren't readable, all u64 big-endian) and its payload, in hex; 32 KiB of records a reply.
- WATCH_CLEAR -> OK | ERR bad_args. The watch and its ring dropped.

Where it records: the first thing pad_get_data does, under pad::g_pad_mutex (cellPadGetData and cellPadPeriphGetData hold it; the verbs take it), on the guest thread making the read. The first read of each flip on any port copies the ranges into the next record (the oldest once the ring is full), so a flip read twice is recorded once, at its first read, and a game that reads its pads once a frame gets one record a frame taken at the same point in it. A range that isn't readable at that moment reads as zeros with its bit set in the mask. A new emulation session (a boot, a savestate's reboot) drops the watch, as O4 drops its schedules.

Touches cellPad.cpp (the watch, the ring, one call at the top of pad_get_data) and Skylander.cpp beside O3 and O4.

